# Copy to deploy/.env on the VM and fill in. **Never committed** - deploy/.env is gitignored,
# and the values below are placeholders that will not work.
#
#   cp deploy/env.example deploy/.env
#
# Every one of these is mandatory. `compose.yml` uses `${VAR:?message}` so a missing value stops
# the deployment with a named error rather than starting something half-configured. The one
# exception is the Slack webhook, which is explicitly allowed to be empty.

# The hostname the certificate is issued for and the URL people open. A bare domain or
# subdomain, no scheme and no trailing slash - Caddy adds https itself.
#   SITE_ADDRESS=faultline.example.com
SITE_ADDRESS=

# **The image CI published, named by its commit.** Never `:latest`.
#
#   ghcr.io/chandanaroyals/faultline:<the full 40-character sha of a commit on main>
#
# A tag that moves means the VM silently changes what it runs on the next `up -d`, and a rollback
# becomes a question about what `:latest` pointed at last Tuesday. This variable *is* the rollback
# mechanism - README §3.6 changes it and brings the platform back up. Find the shas at
# https://github.com/ChandanaRoyalS/faultline/pkgs/container/faultline
FAULTLINE_IMAGE=

# The database password. Not `faultline-dev`: that one is in docker-compose.yml, in this
# repository, on a public GitHub. Generate one and paste it:
#   openssl rand -base64 24
#
# Any characters are safe here. It reaches libpq through PGPASSWORD, never through the DSN -
# see compose.yml. An earlier version interpolated it into the connection URL, where a `/` from
# base64 ended the authority section and repointed the connection at a host nobody configured.
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=

# The credential on the incident read surface. The screen serves every log line the agent quoted
# and every query it ran, so this is the only thing between that and the internet.
#   openssl rand -base64 18
FAULTLINE_API_USER=faultline
FAULTLINE_API_PASSWORD=

# **The same password again, bcrypt-hashed, for Caddy.** Two forms of one secret, because two
# things check it: Faultline checks the plaintext itself in `faultline.api.auth`, and Caddy guards
# Grafana and Jaeger - the demo's UIs, which have no authentication of their own - and takes only a
# hash. Generate it from the password above:
#
#   docker run --rm caddy:2-alpine caddy hash-password --plaintext '<the password>'
#
# Paste the whole `$2a$14$...` string. In a `.env` file it is read literally, so `$` needs no
# escaping here; it would inside a compose `command:`.
FAULTLINE_API_PASSWORD_HASH=

# **The key the orchestrator spends.** This is the one container on the VM that makes model calls,
# and this is the only secret here whose leak costs money directly rather than exposing data.
#
# It is why `POST /api/v1/alerts` returns 404 through Caddy: an alert opens an incident, an
# incident runs an investigation, and an investigation bills this key. The world's own Alertmanager
# reaches the receiver on the compose network instead.
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=

# T5.2's notifications, and **the only optional line in this file.** Empty is a supported state:
# `faultline.notify.slack.from_settings` returns a notifier that sends nothing and says so once,
# and the deployment investigates either way.
FAULTLINE_NOTIFY_SLACK_WEBHOOK_URL=
